Convert Markdown or CSV to ADF JSON, or ADF JSON to Markdown or CSV

Usage: adf convert [OPTIONS] [INPUT]

Arguments:
  [INPUT]  Input file, or `-` for stdin (the default)

Options:
  -o, --output <PATH>        Write the result to PATH (atomically; nothing is written on failure)
      --from <FROM>          Input format: Markdown, ADF JSON (a document or any envelope), or
                             CSV/TSV (becomes a table) [default: md] [possible values: md, adf, csv]
      --to <FORMAT>          Output format when the input is ADF; md is the default (only with
                             --from adf) [possible values: md, csv]
  -e, --envelope <ENVELOPE>  Wrap the document for an API (Markdown and CSV input only; ADF input is
                             detected) [possible values: doc, jira-comment, jira-description,
                             jira-field, confluence]
      --field <NAME>         Field name: for -e jira-field, or which `fields` entry to read from ADF
                             input (default description)
  -t, --target <TARGET>      Product whose rules the ADF must follow; auto: confluence for -e
                             confluence, else jira [default: auto] [possible values: auto, jira,
                             confluence]
      --pretty               Pretty-print JSON (the default on a terminal)
      --compact              Print compact JSON (the default in pipes and files)
      --strict               Fail with E_LOSSY (exit 5) instead of writing a result that has
                             warnings
  -h, --help                 Print help (see more with '--help')

Markdown options:
      --softbreak <MODE>    What a newline inside a paragraph becomes [default: space] [possible
                            values: space, hardbreak]
      --html <POLICY>       What happens to HTML without an ADF equivalent [default: text] [possible
                            values: text, drop, error]
      --images <POLICY>     What happens to images [default: link] [possible values: link, external,
                            drop]
      --extensions <LIST>   ADF extensions to recognise: all, none, or a comma-separated list
                            (alerts,details,cells,raw-adf,mention,status,date,emoji,card,media,csv)
                            [default: all]
      --include-root <DIR>  Let ![alt](adf:csv/PATH) read CSV files under DIR and include them as
                            tables. Off by default; no directory is ever read without this flag. The
                            Markdown decides which files are read: DIR must hold only data you are
                            willing to publish (never a home directory, a repository root or a
                            directory with secrets). Markdown input only

CSV options:
      --delimiter <DELIM>  Field separator: comma (default), tab, semicolon, pipe, or one printable
                           ASCII character such as '|' (--from csv, --to csv)
      --no-header          The first CSV row is data: the header becomes "Column 1", "Column 2", …
                           (--from csv)
      --max-rows <N>       Keep only the first N data rows, with W_CSV_TRUNCATED when more existed
                           (--from csv)
      --align-numbers      Right-align columns whose cells are all numbers (--from csv)
      --table <N>          Which table of the document to write, counting from 1 in document order
                           (--to csv)
      --csv-safe           Prefix cells that start with = + - @ (a spreadsheet formula) with ' (--to
                           csv)

ADF → Markdown options:
      --md-flavor <FLAVOR>  Markdown flavour of ADF → Markdown output [default: roundtrip] [possible
                            values: roundtrip, plain]

Global options:
      --error-format <FORMAT>  How diagnostics are printed on stderr [default: auto] [env:
                               ADF_ERROR_FORMAT] [possible values: auto, text, json]
  -q, --quiet                  Do not print warnings (errors are always printed; exit codes are
                               unchanged)
      --no-color               Never use colour (also: the NO_COLOR environment variable)
